y         Please see him as early as            I would appreciate it if\n       as possible and advise him on           possible and advise him on             you could see him as early as\n       further management.                     further management.(this               possible and advise him on\n      I will be pleased if you can            sounds like an order so should         further management.\n       examine, diagnose and treat             not be used)                          It would be greatly\n       the patient as you feel                Please examine, diagnose and           appreciated if you could see\n       appropriate.                            treat the patient as you feel          him as early as possible and\n      Can you take over her care for          appropriate.                           advise him on further\n       appropriate treatment?                 Please take over her care for          management. (passive and\n                                               appropriate treatment.                 most polite)\n                                                                                     I would be grateful if\n                                                                                      you could examine, diagnose\n                                                                                      and treat the patient as you\n                                                                                      feel appropriate. (active)\n                                                                                     I would appreciate it\n                                                                                      if you could examine, diagnose\nExplanation: These sentences are                                                      and treat the patient as you\nfine in spoken English, but not                                                       feel appropriate. (active)\nsuitable for formal writing. Can and    Explanation: These sentences are             I would appreciate it if you\nwill are considered less polite         acceptable in written English, but can        could take over her care for\nthan could and would.                   sound quite direct.                           appropriate treatment.", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Royal Melbourne Eye Hospital.\nHusband will drive to hospital\n\nHandy Hint\nWhen describing the history, base your letter from today’s perspective. That means, include all the relevant past\nhistory, but in summary form, eliminating less important detail and focus your attention on the current situation\nwhich is usually more important.\n\nConclusions\n\nThe conclusion or final paragraph in the letter should be fairly standard in structure. It should be based on the task\nquestion which is found at the end of case notes. It may contain one or two of the following points:\n\n\n\n\n\n\na polite request of action required\na summary of your (suspected) diagnosis\na thank you for ongoing support\nan offer of future assistance if required (this can be useful if you choose to omit some details from the case\nnotes)\n\nIt is useful to be familiar with some standard patterns so that you are able to conclude your letter confidently,\nquickly and most importantly, accurately. However, some degree of originality will impress the assessors.\nTherefore, where possible try to ensure that your conclusion is related to your task and not simply a memorized\nending.\nHandy hint\nWhen describing the chief complaint or your diagnosis, try to use different words or sentence structure to what you\nhave said in the introduction.\n\nImportant Grammar Rules\n\nModal Verbs: Some modal verbs & the verb hope are used to convey politeness and commonly used for polite\nrequests in the conclusion of formal letters. Compare the patterns below.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInformal\nI want you to see him as early\nas possible and advise him on\nfurther management.\nI will be pleased if you can\nexamine, diagnose and treat\nthe patient as you feel\nappropriate.\nCan you take over her care for\nappropriate treatment?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nPolite\nPlease see him as early as\npossible and advise him on\nfurther management.(this\nsounds like an order so should\nnot be used)\nPlease examine, diagnose and\ntreat the patient as you feel\nappropriate.\nPlease take over her care for\nappropriate treatment.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nExplanation: These sentences are\nfine in spoken English, but not\nsuitable for formal writing. Can and\nwill are considered less polite\nthan could and would.\n\nExplanation: These sentences are\nacceptable in written English, but can\nsound quite direct.\n\n\n\nMore polite\nI would appreciate it if\nyou could see him as early as\npossible and advise him on\nfurther management.\nIt would be greatly\nappreciated if you could see\nhim as early as possible and\nadvise him on further\nmanagement. (passive and\nmost polite)\nI would be grateful if\nyou could examine, diagnose\nand treat the patient as you\nfeel appropriate. (active)\nI would appreciate it\nif you could examine, diagnose\nand treat the patient as you\nfeel appropriate. (active)\nI would appreciate it if you\ncould take over her care for\nappropriate treatment.", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 612.0, "height": 792.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Royal Melbourne Eye Hospital.\nHusband will drive to hospital", "lines": [{"text": "Royal Melbourne Eye Hospital.", "x": 0.03137254901960784, "y": 0.019027272727272726, "width": 0.2501388235294118, "height": 0.015463636363636365, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Royal", "x": 0.03137254901960784, "width": 0.045261176470588235, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Melbourne", "x": 0.08267843137254903, "width": 0.08544941176470587, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Eye", "x": 0.17361254901960785, "width": 0.029795294117647076, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Hospital.", "x": 0.20889254901960785,