d Mr. Smith              years.                                   years(..by Dr X).\n          at this hospital for 3 years.\n                                              •   Dr X has been treating Mr.           •   They have been treated at\n                                                  Smith at this hospital for 3             this hospital for 3 years(..by\n                                                  years.                                   Dr X).\n\n\n\nPresent perfect is often combined with for/since/over. Compare the usage below and note time markers used.\n\nFor                                       Since                                    Over\n\nFor is used to describe a period of       Since is used to refer to the start of   Over uses similar time markers to\ntime                                      period of time                           for but is used to express a change\n                                                                                   in condition, or to describe a\n      •   I have been treating the            •   I have been treating the         repeated event.\n          patient for 3 years.                    patient since 2007.\n                                                                                       •   The patient's condition has\n      •   The patient has been on this        •   The patient has been on this             deteriorated over the past 3\n          medication for 6 months.                medication since January.                months.(change in\n      •   I haven’t seen the patient          •   I haven’t seen this patient              condition)\n          for 1 year.                             since December 2009.                 •   The patient has presented\n      •   The patient has been in pain        •   The patient has been in pain             several times over the last\n          for 5 hours.                            since 10am.                              year. (repeated event)\n\n      •   The patient has been                •   The patient has been                 •   The patient's back pain has\n          waiting for 15 minutes.                 waiting since 11.45am.                   worsened over the last\n                                                                                           week.(change in condition)\n      •   He has been complaining of          •   He has been complaining of\n          back pain for a week.                   back pain since last week.           •   He has tried to quit smoking\n                                                                                           several times over the past\n                                                                                           25 years.(repeated event)", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Writing\n\nPresent Perfect\n\nPresent perfect tense is used to describe an event which starts in the past and continues until the present.\nMastery of this pattern is an essential for writing successful referral letters. Three common ways to form present\nperfect when writing referral letters are as follows:\n\nPresent Perfect Simple\n\nPresent Perfect Progressive\n\nPresent Perfect Passive\n\nForm: have/has+ past participle\n\nForm: have/has + been +____ing\n(present participle)\n\nForm: have/has +been+ past\nparticiple\n\n•\n\n•\n\nI have treated Mr. Smith at\nthis hospital for 3 years.\n\n•\n\nI have been treating Mr.\nSmith at this hospital for 3\nyears.\n\n•\n\nMr. Smith has been treated\nat this hospital for 3\nyears(..by Dr X).\n\n•\n\nDr X has been treating Mr.\nSmith at this hospital for 3\nyears.\n\n•\n\nThey have been treated at\nthis hospital for 3 years(..by\nDr X).\n\nDr X has treated Mr. Smith\nat this hospital for 3 years.\n\nPresent perfect is often combined with for/since/over . Compare the usage below and note time markers used.\n\nFor\n\nSince\n\nOver\n\nFor is used to describe a period of\ntime\n\nSince is used to refer to the start of\nperiod of time\n\nOver uses similar time markers to\nfor but is used to express a change\nin condition, or to describe a\nrepeated event.\n\n•\n\nI have been treating the\npatient for 3 years.\n\n•\n\nI have been treating the\npatient since 2007.\n\n•\n\nThe patient has been on this\nmedication for 6 months.\n\n•\n\nThe patient has been on this\nmedication since January.\n\n•\n\nI haven’t seen the patient\nfor 1 year.\n\n•\n\nI haven’t seen this patient\nsince December 2009.\n\n•\n\nThe patient has been in pain\nfor 5 hours.\n\n•\n\nThe patient has been in pain\nsince 10am.\n\n•\n\nThe patient has been\nwaiting for 15 minutes.\n\n•\n\nThe patient has been\nwaiting since 11.45am.\n\n•\n\nHe has been complaining of\nback pain for a week.\n\n•\n\nHe has been complaining of\nback pain since last week.\n\n•\n\nThe patient's condition has\ndeteriorated over the past 3\nmonths.(change in\ncondition)\n\n•\n\nThe patient has presented\nseveral times over the last\nyear. (repeated event)\n\n•\n\nThe patient's back pain has\nworsened over the last\nweek.(change in condition)\n\n•\n\nHe has tried to quit smoking\nseveral times over the past\n25 years.(repeated event)", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 612.0, "height": 792.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Writing", "lines": [{"text": "Writing", "x": 0.09647058823529411, "y": 0.048939393939393935, "width": 0.05595764705882353, "height": 0.013939393939393939, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Writing", "x": 0.09647058823529411, "width": 0.05595764705882353, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "Present Perfect", "lines": [{"text": "Present Perfect", "x": 0.09568627450980392, "y": 0.08261363636363638, "width": 0.14592882352941175, "height": 0.017727272727272717, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Present", "x": 0.09568627450980392, "width": 0.07279235294117646, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Perfect", "x": 0.17366333333333334, "width": 0.06795176470588235, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "Present perfect tense is 