e": 1}]}, {"text": "that her home ovulation prediction test", "x": 0.5608403361344537, "y": 0.8839762470308788, "width": 0.31185882352941174, "height": 0.015776722090261274, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "that", "x": 0.5608403361344537, "width": 0.030292436974789918, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "her", "x": 0.5961142857142857, "width": 0.025714285714285735, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "home", "x": 0.6268705882352941, "width": 0.04473277310924367, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "ovulation", "x": 0.6767462184873949, "width": 0.07611428571428573, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "prediction", "x": 0.7579025210084034, "width": 0.08166050420168071, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "test", "x": 0.844746218487395, "width": 0.0279529411764705, "confidence": 1}]}]}]}}}, {"number": 180, "text": "than two simple sentences.\n                                                   showed positive.\n\n\n   •   The patient has had placement of a      •   The patient had placement of a\n       prosthetic heart valve 12 months ago.       prosthetic heart valve 12 months ago.\n\nExplanation: has had is present perfect, but\nsimple past is required with the time marker\nago.\n\n   •   I first saw Mrs. Smythe at my clinic    •   When I first saw Mrs. Smythe at my\n       last week , she urgently needed a new       clinic last week , she urgently needed\n       partial denture.                            a new partial denture. (best choice)\n\nExplanation: An adverb of time such as when    •   I first saw Mrs. Smythe at my clinic\nrequired to connect these two independent          last week and she urgently needed a\nclauses                                            new partial denture.", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 181, "text": "Articles Usage with Countable & Uncountable Nouns\n\nWhether an article is required or not depends on the noun that follows. For this purpose\nnouns can be classified into two types: countable & uncountable nouns. The difference is\nbased on whether the noun can be counted or not. For example, complaint is a countable noun\nas it can be counted. Therefore, it is possible to say 1 complaint or 2 complaints. Advice on\nthe other hand cannot be counted so it is not possible to say 1 advice or 2 advices , The\ncorrect expression is some/any advice or the expression a piece of advice.\n\nCountable nouns\n\nThese nouns have a singular form and plural form.\n\nSingular\n\nIn the singular form an article is usually used before the noun. Example: The doctor received\na complaint from her patient.\n\nNB. Exceptions: The article can be left out if it is replaced with another determiner such as\nhis/her or this /that or any/each/every. E.g. The doctor listened to each complaint. Her\ncomplaint was recorded.\n\nPlural\n\nIn the plural form the article is usually not used before the noun. Example: The doctor\nreceived complaints from her patients.\n\n  Remember: Always use articles when referring a particular job such as:\ndoctor/dentist/nurse/pharmacist/physiotherapist/teacher etc etc.\n\n   Countable nouns                   Singular form                       Plural form\n                                Note the use of an article before Note the absence of the\n                                 each noun in singular form, indefinite articles a/an before\n                                       either as a/an/the         each noun in singular form, but\n                                                                  the definite article\nabscess                         The patient had an abscess on The patient had 3 abscesses on\n                                her gum.                      her gum.\nache                            The patient reported a dull      The patient suffered from\n                                ache in her abdomen.             aches and pains.\naccountant                      Mr. Hagen is an accountant.      Mr. & Mrs. Hagen are both\n                                                                 accountants.\nappointment                     A follow-up appointment was The patient did not attend her\n                                scheduled.                  follow-up appointments.\ncavity                          The cavity was exposed.          The cavities were exposed.\ncheck up                        The patient attended for a       Regular check-ups will keep\n                                check-up.                        you healthy.", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Articles Usage with Countable & Uncountable Nouns\n\nWhether an article is required or not depends on the noun that follows. For this purpose\nnouns can be classified into two types: countable & uncountable nouns. The difference is\nbased on whether the noun can be counted or not. For example, complaint is a countable noun\nas it can be counted. Therefore, it is possible to say 1 complaint or 2 complaints. Advice on\nthe other hand cannot be counted so it is not possible to say 1 advice or 2 advices , The\ncorrect expression is some/any advice or the expression a piece of advice.\n\nCountable nouns\n\nThese nouns have a singular form and plural form.\n\nSingular\n\nIn the singular form an article is usually used before the noun. Example: The doctor received\na complaint from her patient.\n\nNB. Exceptions: The article can be left out if it is replaced with another determiner such as\nhis/her or this /that or any/each/every. E.g. The doctor listened to each complaint. Her\ncomplaint was recorded.\n\nPlural\n\nIn the plural form the article is usually not used before the noun. Example: The doctor\nreceived complaints from her patients.\n\nRemember: Always use articles when referring a particular job such as:\ndoctor/dentist/nurse/pharmacist/physiotherapist/teacher etc etc.\n\nCountable nouns\n\nSingular form\n\nPlural form\n\nNote the use of an article before Note the absence of the\neach noun in singular form, indefinite articles a/an before\neither as a/an/the\neach noun in singular form, but\nthe definite article\n\nabscess\n\nThe patient had an abscess on The patient had 3 abscesses on\nher gum.\nher gum.\n\nache\n\nThe patient reported a dull\nache in her abdomen.\n\nThe patient suffered from\naches and pains.\n\naccountant\n\nMr. Hagen is an accountant.\n\nMr. & Mrs. Hagen are both\naccountants.\n\nappointment\n\nA follow-up appointment was The patient did not attend her\nscheduled.\nfollow-up appointments.\n\ncavity\n\nThe cavity was exposed.\n\nThe cavities were exposed.\n\ncheck up\n\nThe patient attended for a\ncheck-up.\n\nRegular check-ups will keep\nyou healthy.", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 595.0, "height": 842.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Articles Usage with Countable & Uncountable Nouns", "lines": [{"text": "Articles Usage with Countable & Uncountable Nouns", "x": 0.26873949579831935, "y": 0.10274109263657957, "width": 0.4578756302521009, "height": 0.01577672209026129, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Articles", "x": 0.26873949579831935, "width": 0.06713949579831933, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Usage", "x": 0.3409613445378151, "width": 0.05144873949579831, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "with", "x": 0.3975529411764706, "width": 0.03807731092436971, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Countable", "x": 0.4405512605042017, "width": 0.08952605042016812, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "&", "x": 0.5352201680672269, "width": 0.016799999999999968, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Uncountable", "x": 0.5570621848739495, "width": 0.10969411764705887, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Nouns", "x": 0.6717579831932774, "width": 0.054857142857142834, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "Whether an article is required or not depends on the noun that follows. For this purpose\nnouns can be classified into two types: countable & uncountable nouns. The difference is\nbased on whether the noun can be counted or not. For example, complaint is a countable noun\nas it can be counted. Therefore, it is possible to say 1 complaint or 2 complaints. Advice on\nthe other hand canno