s a day for the past 25-30", "x": 0.5608403361344537, "y": 0.8924085510688836, "width": 0.2731563025210084, "height": 0.015776722090261274, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "cigarettes", "x": 0.5608403361344537, "width": 0.07722352941176476, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "a", "x": 0.643146218487395, "width": 0.008954621848739453, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "day", "x": 0.6571025210084034, "width": 0.0293848739495798, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "for", "x": 0.6911865546218486, "width": 0.023515966386554652, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "the", "x": 0.719744537815126, "width": 0.02472605042016805, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "past", "x": 0.749472268907563, "width": 0.032490756302521, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "25-30", "x": 0.7869445378151261, "width": 0.047052100840336106, "confidence": 1}]}]}]}}}, {"number": 193, "text": "day for the past 25 – 30 years.               years. (present perfect progressive)\n\n   •   Over the past week she remains free       •   Over the past week she has remained\n       from severe pain and has been able to         free from severe pain and has been\n       tolerate a fluid diet.                        able to tolerate a fluid diet.(present\n                                                     perfect simple)\n   •   Also, there is an ulcer on the right\n       lower lateral border of the tongue        •   Also, there is an ulcer on the right\n       which is present for more than one            lower lateral border of the tongue,\n       year.                                         which has been present for more than\n                                                     one year. (present perfect passive)\n   •   Mr. Diamond is a patient of mine\n       since 2000                                •   Mr Diamond has been a patient of\n                                                     mine since 2000.(present perfect\n   •   Mr. Eddy is a known smoker for 25-            simple)\n       30 years.\n                                                 •   Mr. Eddy has been a smoker for 25\nExplanation: The verbs in these sentences are        years. (present perfect simple)\nin simple present tense. Present perfect needs\nto be used because you are referring to a time\nperiod which started in the past and has\ncontinued to the present.\n\n   •   Mrs. Brown has been presenting to me      •   Mrs. Brown has presented to me on\n       on several occasions over the past few        several occasions over the past few\n       months.                                       months.\n\nExplanation: Progressive form not required.\n\nLast modified: Wednesday, 11 August 2010, 11:00 PM", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 194, "text": "Past Perfect\nPast Perfect is an important tense in referral letters. The main functions of this tense are:\n\n    •   When used with simple past it allows the writer to distinguish the order of events:\n\n            o She had not been able to conceive over the previous four months and as a\n              result she was suffering from depression. This means: first she could not\n              conceive, then she became depressed.\n\n    •   When used in reported speech. The case notes may describe the patient's condition at\n        a time in the past, i.e patient found blood in toilet bowl 2 times. This can be written in\n        the referral letter as follows:\n\n            o At today’s consultation, Ms. Leon reported that there had been blood in the\n              toilet bowl on 2 occasions.\n\nSo the main benefits of using past perfect is that it allows the writer to express the order in\nwhich certain health events occurred logically and clearly.\n\n  Handy Hint 1\nDo not use past perfect when describing one past event as it is not necessary.\nCompare\n\n    •   The patient had been feeling unwell last week.(incorrect if 1 past event described)\n\n    •   The patient was feeling unwell last week. (correct)\n\n    •   The patient had been feeling unwell last week and was admitted to hospital for\n        observation. (correct as 2 past events need to be distinguished)\n\n   Handy Hint 2\nPast perfect is often used with the word previous instead of ago to demonstrate that you are\nreferring to a time before a particular date in the past, not the date you are writing the letter.\nSee below for examples.\n                    Incorrect                                          Correct\n\n    •   She presented to me on 03/07/2010                •   She presented to me on 03/07/2010 for\n        for a regular check up because she                   a regular check up because she had\n        experienced several episode of heart                 experienced several episodes of heart\n        flutter over the past few weeks.                     flutter over the previous few weeks.\n\n            o Past perfect tense is necessary here\n                (had experienced) to differentiate\n                what happened prior to the check up,\n                which is also in the past.\n\n            o As your meaning is prior to 03/07/10\n                and not the day of writing the letter,\n                you should use the word previous", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Past Perfect\n\nPast Perfect is an important tense in referral letters. The main functions of this tense are:\n\n•\n\nWhen used with simple past it allows the writer to distinguish the order of events:\n\no She had not been able to conceive over the previous four months and as a\nresult she was suffering from depression. This means: first she could not\nconceive, then she became depressed.\n\n•\n\nWhen used in reported speech. The case notes may describe the patient's condition at\na time in the past, i.e patient found blood in toilet bowl 2 times. This can be written in\nthe referral letter as follows:\n\no At today’s consultation, Ms. Leon reported that there had been blood in the\ntoilet bowl on 2 occasions.\n\nSo the main benefits of using past perfect is that it allows the writer to express the order in\nwhich certain health events occurred logically and clearly.\n\nHandy Hint 1\nDo not use past perfect when describing one past event as it is not necessary.\nCompare\n\n•\n\nThe patient had been feeling unwell last week.(incorrect if 1 past event described)\n\n•\n\nThe patient was feeling unwell last week. (correct)\n\n•\n\nThe patient had been feeling unwell last week and was admitted to hospital for\nobservation. (correct as 2 past events need to be distinguished)\n\nHandy Hint 2\nPast perfect is often used with the word previous instead of ago to demonstrate that you are\nreferring to a time before a particular date in the past, not the date you are writing the letter.\nSee below for examples.\nIncorrect\nCorrect\n\n•\n\nShe presented to me on 03/07/2010\nfor a regular check up because she\nexperienced several episode of heart\nflutter over the past few weeks.\n\no Past perfect tense is necessary here\n\n(had experienced) to differentiate\nwhat happened prior to the check up,\nwhich is also in the past.\n\no As your meaning is prior to 03/07/10\n\nand not the day of writing the letter,\nyou should use the word previous\n\n•\n\nShe presented to me on 03/07/2010 for\na regular check up because she had\nexperienced several episodes of heart\nflutter over the previous few weeks.", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 595.0, "height": 842.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Past Perfect", "lines": [{"text": "Past Perfect", "x": 0.12117647058823529, "y": 0.0861894299287411, "width": 0.1164403361344538, "height": 0.01774881235154395, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Past", "x": 0.12117647058823529, "width": 0.04163445378151262, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Perfect", "x": 0.1683470588235294, "width": 0.06926974789915967, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "Past Perfect is an important tense in referral letters. The main functions of this tense are:", "lines": [{"text": "Past Perfect is an important tense in referral letters. The main functions