mber": 197, "text": "Simple Past or Present Perfect\n\n\nThere is often confusion of whether to use simple past or present perfect tense. The basic rule\nto remember is if you are referring a particular time in the past then you must use simple past\ntense. If you are referring to a period of time that starts in the past and continues up to now\nuse present perfect tense.\n\n\nThere are some common time markers used with simple past and present perfect. It is\nimportant to study, learn and use these tenses correctly when writing referral letters as you\nmust refer to both past events and periods of time leading to the present.\n      Time Markers with Simple Past                  Time markers with Present Perfect\n\n   •   He sucked his thumb until he was 5.          •   He has been sucking his thumb for\n                                                        five years.\n   •   Mrs. Kelly had diverticulitis when she\n       was a teenager.                              •   Mrs. Kelly has had diverticulitis for\n                                                        the last 12 years.\n   •   He first came to see me in 2004.\n                                                    •   He has been seeing me since 2004.\n   •   The patient didn’t respond to\n       treatment.                                   •   The patient hasn’t responded to\n                                                        treatment yet.\n   •   The patient was diagnosed with cancer\n       3 months ago.                                •   The patient has shown no signs of\n                                                        improvement up to now.\n   •   The patient stopped taking medication\n       yesterday.                                   •   The patient has stopped taking\n                                                        medication recently.\n\n\n\n                                       Common Errors\n                  Incorrect                                         Correct\n\n   •   She has underwent triple coronary            •   She underwent triple coronary bypass\n       bypass surgery on 10.08.09                       surgery on 10.08.09\n\n\n  This page is still under construction\nLast modified: Saturday, 24 July 2010, 06:09 AM", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Simple Past or Present Perfect\n\nThere is often confusion of whether to use simple past or present perfect tense. The basic rule\nto remember is if you are referring a particular time in the past then you must use simple past\ntense. If you are referring to a period of time that starts in the past and continues up to now\nuse present perfect tense.\n\nThere are some common time markers used with simple past and present perfect. It is\nimportant to study, learn and use these tenses correctly when writing referral letters as you\nmust refer to both past events and periods of time leading to the present.\nTime Markers with Simple Past\nTime markers with Present Perfect\n\n•\n\nHe sucked his thumb until he was 5.\n\n•\n\nHe has been sucking his thumb for\nfive years.\n\n•\n\nMrs. Kelly had diverticulitis when she\nwas a teenager.\n\n•\n\nMrs. Kelly has had diverticulitis for\nthe last 12 years.\n\n•\n\nHe first came to see me in 2004.\n\n•\n\nHe has been seeing me since 2004.\n\n•\n\nThe patient didn’t respond to\ntreatment.\n\n•\n\nThe patient hasn’t responded to\ntreatment yet.\n\n•\n\nThe patient was diagnosed with cancer\n3 months ago.\n\n•\n\nThe patient has shown no signs of\nimprovement up to now.\n\n•\n\nThe patient stopped taking medication\nyesterday.\n\n•\n\nThe patient has stopped taking\nmedication recently.\n\nCommon Errors\n\nIncorrect\n\n•\n\nShe has underwent triple coronary\nbypass surgery on 10.08.09\n\nThis page is still under construction\nLast modified: Saturday, 24 July 2010, 06:09 AM\n\nCorrect\n\n•\n\nShe underwent triple coronary bypass\nsurgery on 10.08.09", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 595.0, "height": 842.0, "blocks