ith his wife\n       a government flat, is an aged care               in a government flat, is an aged care\n       pensioner.                                       pensioner.\n\nExplanation: Commas required as it is a non-        •   Mr. Holmes lives with his wife in a\ndefining relative clause. You can also express          government flat and is an aged care\nthis information in a compound sentence.                pensioner.\n\n\n   •   Mr. O'Riley, who lives alone in his          •   Mr. O'Riley, who lives alone in his\n       own home, and works as a fencing                 own home, works as a fencing\n       contractor and has only one brother.             contractor and has only one brother.\n\nExplanation: After the second comma a verb\nis required. i.e Mr O'Riley works..\n\n   •   Mrs. Peterson who recently moved to          •   Mrs. Peterson recently moved to our\n       our retirement village following her             retirement village following her\n       husband's death.                                 husband's death.\n\nExplanation: No relative pronoun needed here        •   Mrs. Peterson, who recently moved to\nas it is a simple sentence.                             our retirement village following her\n                                                        husband's death, has a history of\n                                                        hypertension.\n\n\n   •   Mr. Brown presented at my clinic             •   Mr. Brown presented at my clinic\n       today with a complaint of fractured              today with a complaint of fractured\n       front teeth in a traumatic car accident.         front teeth which occurred in a\n                                                        traumatic car accident.\nExplanation: A relative clause is required to\ndefine how the teeth were fractured.                •   Mr. Brown presented at my clinic\nAlternatively it can be written as two separate         today with a complaint of fractured\nsentences.                                              front teeth. This occurred in a\n                                                        traumatic car accident.\n\n\n   •   I am writing regarding Mr. Jones, a 35       •   I am writing regarding Mr. Jones, a 35\n       year-old-male, who was recently                  year-old-male who was recently\n       diagnosed with tuberculosis.                     diagnosed with tuberculosis.\n\nExplanation: Second comma not required as it\nis a defining relative clause, i.e it defines Mr.", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 202, "text": "Jones\n\n   •    I am writing to refer Mrs. Margaret        •   I am writing to refer Mrs. Margaret\n        Green, a 66 year old widow with three          Green, a 66 year old widow with three\n        children, with complaints of chest             children who is complaining of chest\n        pain.                                          pain.\n\nExplanation: Relative clause required here as\ntwo prepositional phrases is confusing.\n\n   •    I am writing to refer, Ms. Abbot, a 58     •   I am writing to refer Ms. Abbot, a 58\n        year old widow admitted with pain,             year old widow who was admitted\n        dehydration and nausea.                        with pain, dehydration and nausea.\n\nExplanation: Incorrect comma placement and\nrelative clause required.\n\n   •    Mr. Fisher was admitted to hospital        •   Mr. Fisher was admitted to hospital\n        with the diagnosis of obstructive              with the diagnosis of obstructive\n        artery disease and end with quadruple          artery disease which required\n        artery bi-pass grafts.                         quadruple artery bi-pass grafts.\n\nExplanation: A relative clause is required to\ndefine the treatment.\n\n   •    I am writing to refer this 14-year-old     •   I am writing to refer this 14-year-old\n        boy to you who came to me                      boy who came to me complaining of a\n        complaining of a sprained ankle.               sprained ankle.\n\nExplanation: The relative pronoun must\nfollow the noun it is referring to.\n\n   •    I am writing to refer this patient, a 39   •   I am writing to refer this patient, a 39\n        year old widowed woman, who is                 year old widowed woman who is\n        under our care after being injured in a        under our care after being injured in a\n        car accident.                                  car accident.\n\nExplanation: No comma required as it is a\ndefining relative clause.\n\n   •    Mr. Roberts who is a 72-year-old           •   Mr. Roberts, who is a 72-year-old\n        retired gentleman was admitted to              retired gentleman, was admitted to\n        hospital for acute cerebral infarction         hospital for acute cerebral infarction\n        on 08/03/10.                                   on 08/03/10.\n\nExplanation: Commas required as it is a non-\ndefining relative clause and provides extra", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Jones\n\n•\n\nI am writing to refer Mrs. Margaret\nGreen, a 66 year old widow with three\nchildren, with complaints of chest\npain.\n\n•\n\nI am writing to refer Mrs. Margaret\nGreen, a 66 year old widow with three\nchildren who is complaining of chest\npain.\n\n•\n\nI am writing to refer Ms. Abbot, a 58\nyear old widow who was admitted\nwith pain, dehydration and nausea.\n\n•\n\nMr. Fisher was admitted to hospital\nwith the diagnosis of obstructive\nartery disease which required\nquadruple artery bi-pass grafts.\n\n•\n\nI am writing to refer this 14-year-old\nboy who came to me complaining of a\nsprained ankle.\n\n•\n\nI am writing to refer this patient, a 39\nyear old widowed woman who is\nunder our care after being injured in a\ncar accident.\n\n•\n\nMr. Roberts, who is a 72-year-old\nretired gentleman, was admitted to\nhospital for acute cerebral infarction\non 08/03/10.\n\nExplanation: Relative clause required here as\