61344537, "y": 0.7864703087885986, "width": 0.3112336134453782, "height": 0.015776722090261274, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "The", "x": 0.5608403361344537, "width": 0.03130084033613449, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "patient", "x": 0.5972840336134454, "width": 0.05483697478991594, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "has", "x": 0.6571025210084034, "width": 0.02684369747899156, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "had", "x": 0.6890285714285714, "width": 0.02908235294117652, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "influenza", "x": 0.7231529411764706, "width": 0.0750050420168067, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "for", "x": 0.8033008403361345, "width": 0.023515966386554652, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "three", "x": 0.8318588235294118, "width": 0.040215126050420165, "confidence": 1}]}, {"text": "days.", "x": 0.5608403361344537, "y": 0.8039287410926366, "width": 0.04196974789915973, "height": 0.015776722090261274, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "days.", "x": 0.5608403361344537, "width": 0.04196974789915973, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "Same", "lines": [{"text": "Same", "x": 0.4769747899159664, "y": 0.8906270783847982, "width": 0.047052100840336106, "height": 0.015776722090261274, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Same", "x": 0.4769747899159664, "width": 0.047052100840336106, "confidence": 1}]}]}]}}}, {"number": 216, "text": "Always use the definite article with this word.\n                 Incorrect                                          Correct\n\n   •   She has a family history of same             •   She has a family history of the same\n       disease that had been controlled by              disease that had been controlled by\n       Risperidone.                                     Risperidone.\n\n   •   The children were treated by same            •   The children were treated by the same\n       dentist.                                         dentist.\n\n   •   The medication is same as last time.         •   The medication is the same as last\n                                                        time.\n\n\n\n                                     Articles with Gerunds\nA gerund is the …ing form of a verb which takes on the grammatical function of a noun. It\ncan be the subject or object in a sentence. Usually no article is required with gerunds.\n                   Incorrect                                         Correct\n\n   •   On review today, Mr Walker has               •   On review today, Mr Walker has\n       reduced the smoking from 20 to 10                reduced smoking from 20 to 10\n       cigarettes per day.                              cigarettes per day.\n\n   •   The patient was advised to stop the          •   The patient was advised to stop\n       drinking.                                        drinking.\n\n\n                                Articles and Nominalisation\nNominalisation is a process where verbs can be turned into a \"noun phrase\". For example:The\npatient sucked his thumb until he was five becomes The patient had a habit of thumb sucking\nuntil the age of five.\n\nOften in medical English it necessary to use nominalisation such as: The excessive drinking\nof alcohol will adversely affect your health. The reduction of weight has led to improved\nhealth. It is common in these cases to use an article before the nominalised expression.\n\nThe other benefit of using the style of writing is that it creates a formal tone and allows you\nto summarise details from the case notes.\n\n                  Incorrect                                         Correct\n\n   •   The patient is complaining of                •   The patient is complaining of\n       discomfort during passing of urine.              discomfort during the passing of urine.", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Always use the definite article with this word.\nIncorrect\n\nCorrect\n\n•\n\nShe has a family history of same\ndisease that had been controlled by\nRisperidone.\n\n•\n\nShe has a family history of the same\ndisease that had been controlled by\nRisperidone.\n\n•\n\nThe children were treated by same\ndentist.\n\n•\n\nThe children were treated by the same\ndentist.\n\n•\n\nThe medication is same as last time.\n\n•\n\nThe medication is the same as last\ntime.\n\nArticles with Gerunds\nA gerund is the …ing form of a verb which takes on the grammatical function of a noun. It\ncan be the subject or object in a sentence. Usually no article is required with gerunds.\nIncorrect\nCorrect\n\n•\n\nOn review today, Mr Walker has\nreduced the smoking from 20 to 10\ncigarettes per day.\n\n•\n\nOn review today, Mr Walker has\nreduced smoking from 20 to 10\ncigarettes per day.\n\n•\n\nThe patient was advised to stop the\ndrinking.\n\n•\n\nThe patient was advised to stop\ndrinking.\n\nArticles and Nominalisation\nNominalisation is a process where verbs can be turned into a \"noun phrase\". For example:The\npatient sucked his thumb until he was five becomes The patient had a habit of thumb sucking\nuntil the age of five.\n\nOften in medical English it necessary to use nominalisation such as: The excessive drinking\nof alcohol will adversely affect your health. The reduction of weight has led to improved\nhealth. It is common in these cases to use an article before the nominalised expression.\n\nThe other benefit of using the style of writing is that it creates a formal tone and allows you\nto summarise details from the case notes.\n\nIncorrect\n\n•\n\nThe patient is complaining of\ndiscomfort during passing of urine.\n\nCorrect\n\n•\n\nThe patient is complaining of\ndiscomfort during the passing of urine.", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 595.0, "height": 842.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Always use the definite article with this word.\nIncorrect", "lines": [{"text": "Always use the definite article with this word.", "x": 0.12117647058823529, "y": 0.08611401425178147, "width": 0.3716974789915966, "height": 0.01577672209026129, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Always", "x": 0.12117647058823529, "widt