ncise letter, not a long\n quite strict in this area.                                              letter.\n\n                                                                         Don’t write under 160 words as there may not be sufﬁcient\n                                                                         language to get a B grade.\n\n Omit information which is not directly relevant to your task. This is   Try to put all the information from the case notes into the letter.\n a big trap for many candidates in that they try to write down all the   Your letter will be too long and also poorly organised and difﬁcult\n information from the task sheet. This does not reﬂect reality.          to read\n\n Expand on all acronyms. For example OPG should be written as            Overuse acronyms. You are being tested on you ability to expand\n orthopantamogram, BP as blood pressure and PR as pulse rate &           on case notes so make sure you do.\n hx as history\n\n Provide a simple clear summary of the condition so that a lay           Use too much medical jargon. Remember it is a test of English not\n person could understand                                                 Latin!\n\n Spend time reading the case notes and grouping information which        Start writing without planning your letter. You should allow 15\n are related such as medication, persistant high blood pressure etc      minutes reading case notes and planning the letter.\n etc\n\n\n\n Use synonyms so that you can express the information from the           Copy directly from the case notes without any changes. You are\n case notes in different ways                                            expected to put the information into your own words.\n\n Allow 5 minutes at the end of the test to proof read your work and      Submit the letter without checking for basic mistakes such as\n ﬁx up any mistakes                                                      grammar/spelling.\n\n\n\n\nLast modiﬁed: Sunday, 11 February 2018, 12:28 PM\n\n\n                                                            Return to: Topic 1 ", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Doctors: Writing Course\n\nDos & Don’ts\n\nBelow are a list of simple points to remember on the day of your exam.\n\nDo\nSummarise all the information from the case notes into sections\nsuch as: treatment given and obvious trends, medication, medical\nhistory. This will be both easier to write and read as well as\navoiding repition\n\nDon't\nFollow a strict chronological order as your letter may become too\nlong, difﬁcult to read and will not focus on the main problem and\nrelated factors.\n\nTry to write somewhere between 180 and 200 words for the body\nof the letter. This is the requirement of OET and the assessors are\nquite strict in this area.\n\nWrite over 220 words as it will effect your overall result. You being\ntested on your ability to write a clear concise letter, not a long\nletter.\n\nDon’t write under 160 words as there may not be sufﬁcient\nlanguage to get a B grade.\n\nOmit information which is not directly relevant to your task. This is\na big trap for many candidates in that they try to write down all the\ninformation from the task sheet. This does not reﬂect reality.\n\nTry to put all the information from the case notes into the letter.\nYour letter will be too long and also poorly organised and difﬁcult\nto read\n\nExpand on all acronyms. For example OPG should be written as\northopantamogram, BP as blood pressure and PR as pulse rate &\nhx as history\n\nOveruse acronyms. You are being tested on you ability to expand\non case notes so make sure you do.\n\nProvide a simple clear summary of the condition so that a lay\nperson could understand\n\nUse too much medical jargon. Remember it is a test of English not\nLatin!\n\nSpend time reading the case notes and grouping information which\nare related such as medication, persistant high blood pressure etc\netc\n\nStart writing without planning your letter. You should allow 15\nminutes reading case notes and planning the letter.\n\nUse synonyms so that you can express the information from the\ncase notes in different ways\n\nCopy directly from the case notes without any changes. You are\nexpected to put the information into your own words.\n\nAllow 5 minutes at the end of the test to proof read your work and\nﬁx up any mistakes\n\nSubmit the letter without checking for basic mistakes such as\ngrammar/spelling.\n\nLast modiﬁed: Sunday, 11 February 2018, 12:28 PM\n\nReturn to: Topic 1 ", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 612.0, "height": 792.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Doctors: Writing Course", "lines": [{"text": "Doctors: Writing Course", "x": 0.04092647058823529, "y": 0.06350225252525253, "width": 0.22125816013071897, "height": 0.018493035353535355, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Doctors:", "x": 0.04092647058823529, "width": 0.07844930555555556, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Writing", "x": 0.12446442320261439, "width": 0.06726326470588234, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Course", "x": 0.1968183774509804, "width": 0.06536625326797386, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "Dos & Don’ts", "lines": [{"text": "Dos & Don’ts", "x": 0.4513790849673203, "y": 0.13159147474747476, "width": 0.12641242483660128, "height": 0.020027585858585852, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Dos", "x": 0.4513790849673203, "width": 0.038275566993464044, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "&", "x": 0.4951522875816994, "width": 0.014507032679738492, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Don’ts", "x": 0.5151724346405229, "width": 0.06261907516339871, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "Below are a list of simple points to remember on the day of your exam.", "lines": [{"text": "Below are a list of simple points to remember on the day of your exam.", "x": 0.29244771241830064, "y": 0.16346042424242424, "width": 0.4483384215686274, "height": 0.013352217171717154, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Below", "x": 0.29244771241830064, "width": 0.038874091503