"number": 698, "text": "Articles with Gerunds\nA gerund is the …ing form of a verb which takes on the grammatical function of a noun. It can be the\nsubject or object in a sentence. Usually no article is required with gerunds.\n                           Incorrect                                                      Correct\n\n     On review today, Mr Walker has reduced the smoking             On review today, Mr Walker has reduced smoking from\n     from 20 to 10 cigarettes per day.                              20 to 10 cigarettes per day.\n     The patient was advised to stop the drinking.                  The patient was advised to stop drinking.\n\n\n\n\n                                     Articles and Nominalisation\nNominalisation is a process where verbs can be turned into a \"noun phrase\". For example:The patient\nsucked his thumb until he was ﬁve becomes The patient had a habit of thumb sucking until the age of\nﬁve.\n\nOften in medical English, it is necessary to use nominalisation such as: The excessive drinking of alcohol\nwill adversely affect your health. The reduction of weight has led to improved health. It is common in\nthese cases to use an article before the nominalised expression.\n\nThe other beneﬁt of using the style of writing is that it creates a formal tone and allows you to summarise\ndetails from the case notes.\n\n                           Incorrect                                                      Correct\n\n     The patient is complaining of discomfort during passing        The patient is complaining of discomfort during the\n     of urine.                                                      passing of urine.\n\n\n\nLast modiﬁed: Friday, 19 January 2018, 12:24 PM\n\n\n                                                     Return to: Topic 2 ", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Articles with Gerunds\nA gerund is the …ing form of a verb which takes on the grammatical function of a noun. It can be the\nsubject or object in a sentence. Usually no article is required with gerunds.\n\nIncorrect\n\nCorrect\n\nOn review today, Mr Walker has reduced the smoking\n\nOn review today, Mr Walker has reduced smoking from\n\nfrom 20 to 10 cigarettes per day.\n\n20 to 10 cigarettes per day.\n\nThe patient was advised to stop the drinking.\n\nThe patient was advised to stop drinking.\n\nArticles and Nominalisation\nNominalisation is a process where verbs can be turned into a \"noun phrase\". For example:The patient\nsucked his thumb until he was ﬁve becomes The patient had a habit of thumb sucking until the age of\nﬁve.\n\nOften in medical English, it is necessary to use nominalisation such as: The excessive drinking of alcohol\nwill adversely affect your health. The reduction of weight has led to improved health. It is common in\nthese cases to use an article before the nominalised expression.\n\nThe other beneﬁt of using the style of writing is that it creates a formal tone and allows you to summarise\ndetails from the case notes.\n\nIncorrect\n\nCorrect\n\nThe patient is complaining of discomfort during passing\n\nThe patient is complaining of discomfort during the\n\nof urine.\n\npassing of urine.\n\nLast modiﬁed: Friday, 19 January 2018, 12:24 PM\n\nReturn to: Topic 2 ", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 612.0, "height": 792.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Articles with Gerunds\nA gerund is the …ing form of a verb which takes on the grammatical function of a noun. It can be the\nsubject or object in a sentence. Usually no article is required with gerunds.", "lines": [{"text": "Articles with Gerunds", "x": 0.4045767973856209, "y": 0.05265559595959596, "width": 0.19050656045751632, "height": 0.017803449494949497, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Articles", "x": 0.4045767973856209, "width": 0.06898363071895426, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "with", "x": 0.4784475310457516, "width": 0.037309861111111134, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Gerunds", "x": 0.5206543235294118, "width": 0.07442903431372