8, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "", "x": 0.5590375816993464, "width": 0.014741240196078514, "confidence": 1}]}]}]}}}, {"number": 699, "text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic\n\n\n\n\nInformal & Casual Expressions\nThe written language of English is different to the spoken language in that it is quite formal whereas the\nspoken form is more casual.\nIf spoken language expressions are used in formal letters it affects the “tone and register” of the letter.\nTherefore, it is important to\nmaintain a formal tone and use standard expressions. Below is a list of inappropriate casual expressions\nwith more formal appropriate expressions.\n\nExample Words\n\n Casual     Formal      Casual      Formal Casual          Formal        Casual       Formal      Casual      Formal Casual           Forma\n asks for   requests      think      believe      get       become         so            very        but      however       told      reporte\n\n   say        state        get       receive      got       became        about       regarding     really     greatly      sad      depress\n\n   said      stated        got      received       So      Therefore      a bit        slightly      kid        child        till\n\n   like     including      like     such as      don't       do not      Thanks        Thank-        kids     children      lots       a lot o\n                                                                                        you\n\n\n\n\nExample Sentences\n\n          Inappropriate Casual or Spoken Expression                                          Appropriate Formal Expression\n\n     She refuses to eat solids and prefers ﬂuids only like apple juice\n     and lemonade.                                                                She refuses to eat solids and prefers ﬂuids such as apple ju\n                                                                                  and lemonade.\n     There are several measures which can be taken to reduce the\n     risk of infection like taking a shower in morning, using separate            There are several measures which can be taken to reduce t\n     towel for everyone, changing underwear and bed sheets                        risk of infection including taking a shower in morning, using\n     regularly, vacuuming carpets, keeping the nails short and                    separate towel for everyone, changing underwear and bed\n     washing hand thoroughly.                                                     sheets regularly, vacuuming carpets, keeping the nails shor\n                                                                                  and washing hand thoroughly.\n     She was given general advise for softening her stool, like\n     changing her dietary habit but she was non compliant.                        She was given her general advise for softening her stool\n                                                                                  as changing her dietary habit but she was non compliant.\n Explanation: Like is a casual expression, and the two commonly used\n formal expressions with the same meaning are such as & including.", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 700, "text": "Thanks for seeing Mr. Brown.\n    Thanks for arranging a home visit for this patient. If you have      Thank you for seeing Mr. Brown.\n    any further questions, please feel free to ask me.                   Thank you for arranging a home visit for this patient. If you\n                                                                         have any further questions, please be free to ask me.\nExplanation: Thanks is a casual expression and should be written in\nfull.\n\n\n    In view of the above signs and symptoms I think she is\n    suffering from schizophrenia.                                        In view of the above signs and symptoms I believe she is\n    The patient's family is thinking about a reduction in her            suffering from schizophrenia\n    medication.                                                          The patient's family is considering a reduction in her\n                                                                         medication.\nExplanation: think is informal and preferable expression is either\nbelieve or consider, depending on the context.\n\n\n    I would really appreciate your attention regarding further           I would very much appreciate your attention regarding furth\n    management of this patient.                                          management of this patient.\n                                                                         I would greatly appreciate your attention regarding further\nExplanation: Really is spoken language and should be avoided in          management of this patient.\nformal writing.\n\n\n    About his dietary habits, he eats a large amount of oily and\n    sweet food.                                                          Regarding his dietary habits, he eats a large amount of oily\n    If you require any other information about her condition, please     sweet food.\n    do not hesitate to contact me.                                       If you require any other information regarding her condition\n                                                                         please do not hesitate to contact me.\nExplanation: About is a casual expression, regarding is more suitable.\nLots of is also casual. “A large amount of” is more formal.\n\n\n    She had lots of pain.\n                                                                         She had a lot of pain.\n    He eats lots of oily food.\n                                                                         He eats large amounts of oily food.\nExplanation:Lots is a spoken expression\n\n\n    There was nothing signiﬁcant in his social history.\n                                                                         There were no signiﬁcant ﬁndings in his social history.\nExplanation: Nothing is more commonly a spoken expression which is\nnot suitable for writing.\n\n\n    Please do not hesitate to contact me if u need additional\n    information.                                                         Please do not hesitate to contact me if you need additional\n                                                                         information.\nExplanation: Deﬁnitely no text or SMS language in formal letters.\nReplace with: you\n\n\n    She has got maternal postpartum haemorrhage of 800mls.               She has had maternal postpartum haemorrhage of 800ml.\n    Mr O’ Riley got an appointment for a follow up visit with his        Mr O’ Riley has an appointment for a follow up visit with his\n    general practitioner, Dr. Avril Jensen.                              general practitioner, Dr. Avril Jensen.\n    The mother got sick for a few days.                                  The mother became sick for a few days\n\nExplanation: Got/Get are casual expressions and should generally be\navoided in formal letters.", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 701, "text": "Should you have any concerns regarding them, please do not\n    hesitate to contact me.                                                 Should you have any concerns regarding Mary and her child\nExplanation: The pronoun them sounds casual here. It is much better         please do not hesitate to contact me.\nto use the patient’s name, especially in the ﬁnal sentence of the letter.\n\n\n\n    At the moment, she is weak and disorientated sometimes but              At the moment, she is weak and disorientated at times\n    severe pain has been alleviated.                                        severe pain has been alleviated.\n                                                                            At the moment, she is weak and disorientated on occasion\nExplanation: Sometimes is a casual expression. More formal                  but severe pain has been alleviated.\nexpressions include: at times or on occasions\n\n\n    This pain was exaggerated to cold and hot things\n                                                                            This pain was exaggerated to hot and cold stimuli.\nExplanation: Things is a casual expression and should be avoided\n\n\n    A repeat vaginal examination revealed a very tender right\n    vaginal fornix. But her blood pressure pulse is within normal           A repeat vaginal examination revealed a very tender right\n    range.                                                                  vaginal fornix. However, her blood pressure pulse is within\n                                                                            normal range.\nExplanation: It is not good English to begin a sentence with the\nconjunction but In this case use However, as it is more formal\n\n\n    Her temperature and blood pressure were normal while pulse\n    was elevated at 88. So I recommended that she return today              Her temperature and blood pressure were normal while pul\n    for a blood test and reassessment.                                      was elevated at 88. Therefore, I recommended that she retu\n                                                                            today for a blood test and reassessment.\nExplanation: So is a casual word, you can use it within a sentence, but\nnot to start a sentence in formal writing. Replace with: Therefore,\n\n\n    There was evidence of poor oral hygiene, carious lesions and\n    active periodontal disease too.                                         There was evidence of poor oral hygiene, carious lesions a\n                                                                            active periodontal disease as well.\nExplanation: too is informal, a more more formal expression is as\nwell.\n\n\n    Her husband was upset because, she was not showing interest\n    towards the kids.                                                       Her husband was upset because, she was not showing inte\n    Mark's dad also suffers from this condition.                            towards the children.\n    The teacher spoke to her mum regarding the regular absences             Mark's father also suffers from this condition.\n    from school.                                                            The teacher spoke to her mother regarding the regular\n                                                                            absences from school.\nExplanation: kids, dad, mum are all spoken expressions which are\ninappropriate in formal letters.\n\n\n    She had had termination of pregnancy 10 years back.\n    He is a regular smoker and drinker with the only relevant past          She had had termination of pregnancy 10 years ago.\n    history of left inguinal hernia operation 2 years back.                 He is a regular smoker and drinker with the only relevant pa\n                                                                            history of left inguinal hernia operation 2 years previously\nExplanation: Back is used as a spoken expression not a written\nexpression.", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 702, "text": "He had a habit of thumb sucking till the age of 5.\n                                                                            He had a habit of thumb sucking until the age of 5.\n Explanation: Till is casual, until is formal\n\n\n     She was so anxious.\n                                                                            She was very anxious.\n Explanation: So is informal and subjective, whereas very is more\n formal and objective.\n\n\n     Owen has big tonsils.\n                                                                            Owen has enlarged tonsils\n Explanation: big is informal and does not sound professional.\n\n\n     I am writing to request a follow-up for this patient.\n     Examination revealed that his vitals were stable and left knee         I am writing to request a follow-up visit for this patient.\n     was swollen without effusion.                                          Examination revealed that his vital signs were stable and le\n     Examination revealed normal vitals.                                    knee was swollen without effusion.\n                                                                            Examination revealed that his vital signs were normal.\n Explanation: Vitals and follow up are incomplete expressions,\n commonly used in spoken English, but not suitable for formal writing.\n\n\n     Mrs. Jones is a widow who complained of persistent chest\n                                                                            Mrs. Jones is a widow who complained of persistent chest\n     pain at roughly 1.45pm.\n                                                                            pain at approximately 1.45pm.\n     Miss Roberts started smoking around 6 months ago.\n                                                                            Miss Roberts started smoking approximately 6 months ago\n Roughly and around are casual or vague expressions.\n\n\n     I recommend some investigations to rule out cancer.\n                                                                            I recommend further investigations to rule out cancer.\n     I requested some blood tests.\n                                                                            I requested additional blood tests.\n Explanation: Some is vague and does not sound professional.\n\n\n     Her BMI was 28 and all the rest of the examinations were\n                                                                            Her BMI was 28 and the remainder of the examinations we\n     normal.\n                                                                            normal.\n Explanation: all the rest of sounds informal.\n\n\n     Abdominal examination revealed a small right groin swelling            Abdominal examination revealed a small right groin swellin\n     that’s consistent with inguinal hernia.                                that is consistent with inguinal hernia.\n     Thank you for your expert care and please don't hesitate to            Thank you for your expert care and please do not hesitate t\n     contact us if you require further information.                         contact us if you require further information.\n Explanation: It is best to avoid contractions in formal letters as these\n are used more in spoken English.\n\n\n     In addition, she is just able to tolerate ﬂuids.\n                                                                            In addition, she is only able to tolerate ﬂuids.\n\n Explanation:Just can sound informal at times.\n\n\n\n\nLast modiﬁed: Friday, 19 January 2018, 1:34 PM", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 703, "text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic\n\n\n\n                                               Medical Terminology\nThe medical case notes for the OET exam often contain abbreviations, medical acronyms and technical\nlanguage. The task for the writer is to expand these into full words and sentences. There are 3 reasons\nwhy this is important.\n 1. As the OET is a test of English, you need to demonstrate your ability to transfer technical language in\n    the case notes into standard English.\n 2. For some letters you are may have to write to someone who is not a health professional such as a\n    social worker or family member.\n 3. A referral letter is not a report so the standard conventions of letter writing require a formal style\n    which includes using complete words and sentences.\n\n  Handy Hint: It is a test of English not Latin, so where possible avoid the use of Latin based acronyms\nand words.\n\nHere is a list of common abbreviations used in Australia.\n    Acronym/Short Form             Full Expression           Acronym/Short Form            Full Expression\nAbdo                        Abdomen                      mane                      in the morning\n\nAC                          before meals                 min                       minute\n\nADL                         activities of daily living   NAD                       no abnormality detected\n\n&                           and                          nocte                     in the evening\n\nBD/bid                      2 times a day                OPG                       orthopantomogram\n\nBP                          blood pressure               P/PR                      Pulse/Pulse Rate\n\nBW x-rays                   bite wing x-rays             PMH                       past medical history\n\nC/O                         complains of                 PRN                       as required\n\ncap                         capsule                      QID                       4 times a day\n\nDOB                         Date of Birth                R                         right\n\nECG                         electrocardiograph           tab                       tablet\n\nF/-                         ﬂuoride application          sid                       once a day\n\nFBC                         full Blood Count             TDS/tid                   3 times a day\n\nFTA                         failed to Attend             w/o                       without\n\nIV                          intravenous                  y/yrs                     year\n\nh                           hour                         ? rheumatic fever         possibility of something\n\nhx                          history                      6/h                       6 hourly\n\nL                           left                         +                         positive\n\nlab                         laboratory                   3/12                      3 months", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic\n\nMedical Terminology\n\nThe medical case notes for the OET exam often contain abbreviations, medical acronyms and technical\nlanguage. The task for the writer is to expand these into full words and sentences. There are 3 reasons\nwhy this is important.\n\n1. As the OET is a test of English, you need to demonstrate your ability to transfer technical language in\n\nthe case notes into standard English.\n2. For some letters you are may have to write to someone who is not a health professional such as a\nsocial worker or family member.\n3. A referral letter is not a report so the standard conventions of letter writing require a formal style\nwhich includes using complete words and sentences.\n\nHandy Hint: It is a test of English not Latin, so where possible avoid the use of Latin based acronyms\nand words.\n\nHere is a list of common abbreviations used in Australia.\n\nAcronym/Short Form\n\nFull Expression\n\nAcronym/Short Form\n\nFull Expression\n\nAbdo\n\nAbdomen\n\nmane\n\nin the morning\n\nAC\n\nbefore meals\n\nmin\n\nminute\n\nADL\n\nactivities of daily living\n\nNAD\n\nno abnormality detected\n\n&\n\nand\n\nnocte\n\nin the evening\n\nBD/bid\n\n2 times a day\n\nOPG\n\northopantomogram\n\nBP\n\nblood pressure\n\nP/PR\n\nPulse/Pulse Rate\n\nBW x-rays\n\nbite wing x-rays\n\nPMH\n\npast medical history\n\nC/O\n\ncomplains of\n\nPRN\n\nas required\n\ncap\n\ncapsule\n\nQID\n\n4 times a day\n\nDOB\n\nDate of Birth\n\nR\n\nright\n\nECG\n\nelectrocardiograph\n\ntab\n\ntablet\n\nF/-\n\nﬂuoride application\n\nsid\n\nonce a day\n\nFBC\n\nfull Blood Count\n\nTDS/tid\n\n3 times a day\n\nFTA\n\nfailed to Attend\n\nw/o\n\nwithout\n\nIV\n\nintravenous\n\ny/yrs\n\nyear\n\nh\n\nhour\n\n? rheumatic fever\n\npossibility of something\n\nhx\n\nhistory\n\n6/h\n\n6 hourly\n\nL\n\nleft\n\n+\n\npositive\n\nlab\n\nlaboratory\n\n3/12\n\n3 months", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 612.0, "height": 792.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic", "lines": [{"text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic", "x": 0.04092647058823529, "y": 0.06350225252525253, "width": 0.2743826617647059, "height": 0.018493035353535355, 