onfidence": 1}, {"text": "to", "x": 0.09106669934640522, "width": 0.014124236928104576, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "rule", "x": 0.10915683823529411, "width": 0.026568194444444453, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "6", "x": 0.13969093627450982, "width": 0.008976388888888888, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "above.", "x": 0.1526284362745098, "width": 0.047036596405228746, "confidence": 1}]}]}]}}}, {"number": 712, "text": "Last modiﬁed: Friday, 19 January 2018, 1:25 PM\n\n\n                                                 Return to: Topic 4 ", "extraction": "text"}, {"number": 713, "text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic\n\n\n\n                                                          Letter Format\n\nOne of the criteria used to assess your writing in the OET exam is Control of Presentation Features. This criterion assesses the\noverall presentation of the letter and includes a lot of the features outside the body of the letter including\n\n    Letter format\n    Punctuation\n    Capital letters\n    Spelling\n\nThis worksheet will describe the characteristics of formal medical correspondence. To do this, the letter has been broken up into\n6 parts.\n\n1. Date: The date should be placed at either the top left or top right of the letter and can be either written in full or as numbers as\nfollows:\n\n        21/07/18\n        July 21, 2018\n        21st July, 2018\n\n\n2. Address: The address gives the name and address of the person or facility to whom you are writing. There are some basic\nconventions which need to be followed including:\n\n        Capital letters for job titles, names & street names\n        Commas not required when the address in separated by lines\n\n Dr. John Howard                              Dr Ian Hanson                                Lactation Consultant\n 1 Wickham Tce                                Consultant Orthopaedic Surgeon               Breast Feeding Support Centre\n Spring Hill                                  Riverlea Public Hospital                     68 Main Street\n QLD, 4010                                    Graham Street                                Romaville\n                                              Riverlea                                     N.S.W, 2068\n\n\n\n\n3.Salutation: If the name of the person is included in the case notes then it should be used. This can be followed by either a\ncomma or full colon.\n\n        Dear Dr. Wilson,\n        Dear Dr. Wilson:\n\n\nIf the name of person is not mentioned and they are a non-medical person, then you can begin with Sir/Madam as used in\nbusiness correspondence or if they are a medical professional you can use their job title Doctor/Nurse/Dentist etc\n\n        Dear Sir/Madam,\n        Dear Doctor\n        Dear Nurse\n\n\n4.Subject: The subject is a place where information such as the name & age of the patient can be included. This can save you", "extraction": "text", "display": {"version": 2, "text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic\n\nLetter Format\n\nOne of the criteria used to assess your writing in the OET exam is Control of Presentation Features. This criterion assesses the\noverall presentation of the letter and includes a lot of the features outside the body of the letter including\n\nLetter format\n\nPunctuation\n\nCapital letters\n\nSpelling\n\nThis worksheet will describe the characteristics of formal medical correspondence. To do this, the letter has been broken up into\n6 parts.\n\n1. Date: The date should be placed at either the top left or top right of the letter and can be either written in full or as numbers as\nfollows:\n\n21/07/18\n\nJuly 21, 2018\n\n21st July, 2018\n\n2. Address: The address gives the name and address of the person or facility to whom you are writing. There are some basic\nconventions which need to be followed including:\n\nCapital letters for job titles, names & street names\n\nCommas not required when the address in separated by lines\n\nDr. John Howard\n1 Wickham Tce\nSpring Hill\nQLD, 4010\n\nDr Ian Hanson\nConsultant Orthopaedic Surgeon\nRiverlea Public Hospital\nGraham Street\nRiverlea\n\nLactation Consultant\nBreast Feeding Support Centre\n68 Main Street\nRomaville\nN.S.W, 2068\n\n3.Salutation: If the name of the person is included in the case notes then it should be used. This can be followed by either a\ncomma or full colon.\n\nDear Dr. Wilson,\n\nDear Dr. Wilson:\n\nIf the name of person is not mentioned and they are a non-medical person, then you can begin with Sir/Madam as used in\nbusiness correspondence or if they are a medical professional you can use their job title Doctor/Nurse/Dentist etc\n\nDear Sir/Madam,\n\nDear Doctor\n\nDear Nurse\n\n4.Subject: The subject is a place where information such as the name & age of the patient can be included. This can save you", "layout": {"version": 1, "width": 612.0, "height": 792.0, "blocks": [{"text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic", "lines": [{"text": "Grammar & Vocabular y Clinic", "x": 0.04092647058823529, "y": 0.06350225252525253, "width": 0.2743826617647059, "height": 0.018493035353535355, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Grammar", "x": 0.04092647058823529, "width": 0.08648659150326797, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "&", "x": 0.1325017091503268, "width": 0.013395477124183004, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Vocabular", "x": 0.15098787581699347, "width": 0.09509767810457515, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "y", "x": 0.24825823529411767, "width": 0.010250807189542483, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Clinic", "x": 0.26360177450980393, "width": 0.05170735784313726, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "Letter Format", "lines": [{"text": "Letter Format", "x": 0.4321437908496732, "y": 0.13159147474747476, "width": 0.1354262467320262, "height": 0.020027585858585852, "confidence": 1, "words": [{"text": "Letter", "x": 0.4321437908496732, "width": 0.05927538235294122, "confidence": 1}, {"text": "Format", "x": 0.49693007516339865, "width": 0.07063996241830071, "confidence": 1}]}]}, {"text": "One of the criteria used to assess your writing 